Q: Assigning random value to multiple char A: <p>You call <strong>rand()</strong> only one time and use its result as an index for all players. So all players get the same message <strong>messages [randomIndex]</strong>.</p>
<p>At least you need to call <strong>rand()</strong> three times: each call of <strong>rand()</strong> for each player to select the corresponding message.</p>
<p>If you want that all messages would be used in a random order then you can use for example the following approach as shown below.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;time.h&gt;

int main( void )
{
    enum { N = 3 };

    const char * messages[N] = { &quot;ODD&quot;, &quot;EVEN&quot;, &quot;5X&quot; };
    int indices[N] = { 0, 1, 2 };

    const char *names[N] = { &quot;Player1&quot;, &quot;Player2&quot;, &quot;Player3&quot; };

    srand( ( unsigned int )time( NULL ) );

    for ( int i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        int index1 = rand() % N; 
        int index2 = rand() % N;

        if ( index1 != index2 )
        {
            int tmp = indices[index1];
            indices[index1] = indices[index2];
            indices[index2] = tmp;
        }
    }


    for ( int i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s: %s\n&quot;, names[i], messages[indices[i]] );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The program output might look the following way for different runs of the program.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<pre><code>Player1: EVEN
Player2: 5X
Player3: ODD


</code></pre>
</li>
<li>
<pre><code>Player1: ODD
Player2: 5X
Player3: EVEN


</code></pre>
</li>
<li>
<pre><code>Player1: 5X
Player2: ODD
Player3: EVEN
</code></pre>
<p>Or you could write a separate function like</p>
<pre><code>void shuffle( int indices[], int n )
{
  for ( int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++ )
  {
      int index1 = rand() % n; 
      int index2 = rand() % n;

      if ( index1 != index2 )
      {
          int tmp = indices[index1];
          indices[index1] = indices[index2];
          indices[index2] = tmp;
      }
  }
}
</code></pre>
</li>
</ul>
